  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "43788592",
      "title": "TypeScript: Type Aliases & Interfaces",
      "lectureOrder": 41,
      "videoOrder": 37,
      "sourceHash": "7e1b49a907cc99580d8249671b9b95011e667cb64cb72331d4158970dec91e34",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 0,
          "end": 2.43,
          "en": "[Maximilian Schwarzmüller] Now since certain types,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 2.43,
          "end": 5.07,
          "en": "like this object type here, for example,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 5.07,
          "end": 9.27,
          "en": "can become rather complex and a bit longer,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 9.27,
          "end": 13.17,
          "en": "it is also quite common to outsource them",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 13.17,
          "end": 18.15,
          "en": "and to not define them in line as I'm doing it here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 18.15,
          "end": 21.03,
          "en": "And you can do that with TypeScript",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 21.03,
          "end": 24.42,
          "en": "by grabbing the type you want to outsource,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 24.42,
          "end": 27.843,
          "en": "and by creating a so-called type alias.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 28.77,
          "end": 30.96,
          "en": "You do that with the type keyword,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 30.96,
          "end": 34.563,
          "en": "which is a keyword made available by TypeScript,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 35.43,
          "end": 38.73,
          "en": "and then you pick any type name of your choice.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 38.73,
          "end": 40.59,
          "en": "For example, user.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 40.59,
          "end": 42.48,
          "en": "And the convention is to start",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 42.48,
          "end": 44.283,
          "en": "with an uppercase character here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 45.54,
          "end": 48.51,
          "en": "Then you use an equal sign, but as a value,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 48.51,
          "end": 50.7,
          "en": "you now assign a type definition",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 50.7,
          "end": 53.553,
          "en": "because this is a pure TypeScript feature.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 55.2,
          "end": 58.803,
          "en": "So in this case, this user object type I just cut.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 60.18,
          "end": 62.76,
          "en": "And now you can use your own type here,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 62.76,
          "end": 65.37,
          "en": "just type alias, user in my case,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 65.37,
          "end": 70.23,
          "en": "in all the places where this type is expected and required,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 70.23,
          "end": 71.523,
          "en": "like this now.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 73.2,
          "end": 76.23,
          "en": "Now when you are dealing with an object type",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 76.23,
          "end": 78.27,
          "en": "as we do it here,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 78.27,
          "end": 82.71000000000001,
          "en": "you also have an alternative to setting up a type like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 82.71000000000001,
          "end": 84.93,
          "en": "You can create a so-called interface,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 84.93,
          "end": 88.05,
          "en": "which is another TypeScript feature.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 88.05,
          "end": 90.66,
          "en": "And an interface is simply another way",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 90.66,
          "end": 94.11,
          "en": "of defining an object type, you could say.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 94.11,
          "end": 97.25999999999999,
          "en": "And you'll see both approaches out there in the wild,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 97.25999999999999,
          "end": 101.07,
          "en": "which is why I will show you both approaches here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 101.07,
          "end": 104.94,
          "en": "Now the question whether to use a type or an interface",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 104.94,
          "end": 107.91,
          "en": "is a question that can lead to a long discussion.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 107.91,
          "end": 110.7,
          "en": "The short answer is that in many situations",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 110.7,
          "end": 113.97,
          "en": "it doesn't matter and you can use what you prefer.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 113.97,
          "end": 115.469,
          "en": "In Angular projects,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 115.469,
          "end": 118.8,
          "en": "it's a bit more common to see interfaces,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 118.8,
          "end": 121.023,
          "en": "but ultimately it is down to you.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 122.1,
          "end": 126.12,
          "en": "If you were to define such a object type as an interface,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 126.12,
          "end": 129.72,
          "en": "you would do that by using the interface keyword",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 129.72,
          "end": 133.713,
          "en": "and then by again, using any name of your choice, like user.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 134.7,
          "end": 139.53,
          "en": "And then without an equal sign, you add curly braces",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 139.53,
          "end": 142.77,
          "en": "and then your property definitions.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 142.77,
          "end": 144.933,
          "en": "So like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 146.28,
          "end": 148.92000000000002,
          "en": "And now you would have the same result as before",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 148.92000000000002,
          "end": 151.82999999999998,
          "en": "just with this different approach.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 151.82999999999998,
          "end": 155.43,
          "en": "Again, you would have your own object type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 155.43,
          "end": 158.76,
          "en": "One key difference to the type keyword is",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 158.76,
          "end": 163.76,
          "en": "that with interface you can really only define object types.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 163.95,
          "end": 168.21,
          "en": "With the type keyword, you can also define other types",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 168.21,
          "end": 171.48,
          "en": "of which you'll see some later in this course.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 171.48,
          "end": 173.25,
          "en": "And therefore, now with that,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 173.25,
          "end": 175.98,
          "en": "we got the same application as before.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 175.98,
          "end": 177.84,
          "en": "It still works as before,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 177.84,
          "end": 180.6,
          "en": "but now you learn a bit more about TypeScript",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 180.6,
          "end": 183.27,
          "en": "and you will need that knowledge",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 183.27,
          "end": 184.74,
          "en": "for the rest of this course,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 184.74,
          "end": 186.33,
          "en": "and also most importantly,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 186.33,
          "end": 189.063,
          "en": "when working as an Angular developer.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}
